🌐 ASP.NET MVC Fundamentals (Beginner-Friendly)
1. MVC Architecture
Model → Represents data and business logic (like a database table or domain object).

View → UI layer (HTML, CSS, Razor syntax).

Controller → Handles requests, processes input, and returns responses.

👉 Analogy: Think of a restaurant.

Model = Kitchen (ingredients + recipes).

Controller = Waiter (takes orders, communicates between kitchen and customer).

View = Plate of food (presentation to customer).

2. Routing
Defines how URLs map to controllers/actions.

Default route: /{controller}/{action}/{id}

Example: /Home/Index/5 → HomeController.Index(int id = 5)

3. Action Methods
Controller methods that handle requests.

Can return:

View() → HTML page

Json() → JSON data

RedirectToAction() → Redirect to another action

4. Model Binding
Automatically maps HTTP request data (query string, form data, route values) to method parameters or model objects.

Example:

csharp
public ActionResult Save(Student student)
If form fields match Student properties, MVC binds them automatically.

5. Dependency Injection (DI)
Technique to achieve loose coupling.

Instead of creating objects directly (new Service()), we inject them via constructors.

ASP.NET Core MVC has built-in DI; classic MVC uses IoC containers like Unity, Autofac, Ninject.

6. Filters
Allow cross-cutting concerns (logging, authentication, caching).

Types:

Authorization filters

Action filters

Result filters

Exception filters

7. View Engine (Razor)
Razor syntax: @Model.Name, @Html.TextBoxFor(m => m.Email)

Combines C# with HTML.

8. State Management
HTTP is stateless, so we use:

Session

Cookies

TempData

ViewBag / ViewData

9. Validation
Data Annotations: [Required], [StringLength], [Range]

Client-side + server-side validation.

10. Security
Authentication (Forms, Identity, OAuth).

Authorization ([Authorize] attribute).

Anti-forgery tokens (@Html.AntiForgeryToken()).

🎯 25 ASP.NET MVC Interview Questions with Long Answers
Here’s the interview-style Q&A with detailed explanations:

1. What is ASP.NET MVC and why do we use it?
ASP.NET MVC is a framework for building web applications using the Model-View-Controller pattern. It separates concerns:

Model → Business logic & data

View → UI

Controller → Request handling

This separation makes applications easier to test, maintain, and scale. Unlike Web Forms, MVC gives full control over HTML, JavaScript, and CSS, which is crucial for modern web development.

2. Explain the MVC request lifecycle.
Request enters via URL.

Routing maps URL to controller/action.

Controller executes action method.

Model binding maps request data to parameters.

Filters run (authorization, logging).

Action result is generated (View, JSON, Redirect).

View Engine (Razor) renders HTML.

Response sent to client.

3. What is Routing in MVC?
Routing defines how URLs map to controllers/actions. Default route:

csharp
routes.MapRoute(
  name: "Default",
  url: "{controller}/{action}/{id}",
  defaults: new { controller = "Home", action = "Index", id = UrlParameter.Optional }
);
This means /Home/Index/5 → HomeController.Index(5).

4. Explain Model Binding with an example.
Model binding automatically maps form data to objects. Example:

csharp
public ActionResult Register(User user)
If the form has fields Name, Email, Password, MVC binds them to User properties. This avoids manual parsing of Request.Form.

5. What is Dependency Injection (DI) and why is it important?
DI is a design pattern where dependencies are injected rather than created inside a class. Example:

csharp
public class HomeController {
  private readonly IStudentService _service;
  public HomeController(IStudentService service) {
    _service = service;
  }
}
Benefits:

Loose coupling

Easier testing (mocking services)

Better maintainability

6. Difference between ViewData, ViewBag, and TempData.
ViewData → Dictionary, requires type casting.

ViewBag → Dynamic wrapper around ViewData.

TempData → Persists data across redirects (uses session).

7. Explain Filters in MVC.
Filters allow cross-cutting concerns:

Authorization filter → Security checks

Action filter → Pre/post processing

Result filter → Modify result before rendering

Exception filter → Handle errors globally

8. What is Razor View Engine?
Razor allows embedding C# in HTML using @. Example:

html
<p>Hello @Model.Name</p>
It’s lightweight, fast, and prevents XSS by default.

9. Explain Validation in MVC.
Validation uses Data Annotations:

csharp
public class Student {
  [Required]
  public string Name { get; set; }
}
MVC automatically validates and shows errors in views using @Html.ValidationMessageFor.

10. What is the difference between Partial View and Layout View?
Partial View → Reusable UI snippet (like a widget).

Layout View → Master page defining common structure (header, footer).

11. Explain ActionResult types.
ViewResult → Returns HTML

JsonResult → Returns JSON

RedirectResult → Redirects to URL

FileResult → Returns file download

12. What is the difference between ActionResult and JsonResult?
ActionResult → Base class for all results.

JsonResult → Specifically returns JSON data, useful for AJAX calls.

13. Explain Authentication and Authorization in MVC.
Authentication → Verifies identity (login).

Authorization → Grants access based on roles/claims.
Example:

csharp
[Authorize(Roles="Admin")]
public ActionResult Dashboard() { }
14. What is the difference between Session and TempData?
Session → Stores data for entire user session.

TempData → Stores data until it’s read (one request).

15. Explain Bundling and Minification.
Bundling → Combines multiple CSS/JS files into one.

Minification → Removes whitespace/comments to reduce size.
Improves performance.

16. What is the difference between ASP.NET MVC and Web Forms?
MVC → Separation of concerns, full control over HTML, testable.

Web Forms → Event-driven, uses ViewState, less control over HTML.

17. Explain Areas in MVC.
Areas allow modularization of large applications. Each area has its own controllers, views, and routes. Example: Admin, User.

18. What is the difference between Strongly Typed View and Weakly Typed View?
Strongly Typed View → Uses @model directive, compile-time checking.

Weakly Typed View → Uses ViewBag/ViewData, runtime errors possible.

19. Explain Exception Handling in MVC.
Use try-catch in controllers.

Global handling via HandleErrorAttribute.

Custom error pages via web.config.

20. What is the difference between Html.RenderPartial and Html.Partial?
Html.Partial → Returns HTML string.

Html.RenderPartial → Writes directly to response stream (better performance).

21. Explain Dependency Injection in ASP.NET Core MVC.
ASP.NET Core has built-in DI. Services are registered in Startup.cs:

csharp
services.AddScoped<IStudentService, StudentService>();
Injected via constructor.

22. What is Model Validation in MVC?
Model validation ensures data integrity. Example:

csharp
[Range(18, 60)]
public int Age { get; set; }
MVC validates automatically before saving.
